WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.149 --> 00:00:05.000
Speaker 1: Let's go to Miguel from central
Florida.

00:00:05.000 --> 00:00:08.660
Miguel from central Florida, welcome to The
David Pakman Show.

00:00:08.660 --> 00:00:10.030
What can I do for you today?

00:00:10.030 --> 00:00:12.490
Speaker 3: Hi, David.

00:00:12.490 --> 00:00:13.490
How does my mike sound?

00:00:13.490 --> 00:00:14.610
Speaker 1: It sounds beautiful.

00:00:14.610 --> 00:00:17.470
Speaker 3: Oh, perfect.

00:00:17.470 --> 00:00:21.710
I wanted to ask, being someone that's coming
from Central Florida.

00:00:21.710 --> 00:00:27.160
I've obviously seen kind of the the rise of
the Santas and a lot of the rise of the right

00:00:27.160 --> 00:00:28.760
wing culture where issues.

00:00:28.760 --> 00:00:29.760
Yep.

00:00:29.760 --> 00:00:31.710
So I was wondering.

00:00:31.710 --> 00:00:37.040
Considering DeSantis expanded his lead in.

00:00:37.040 --> 00:00:38.280
Florida this time around.

00:00:38.280 --> 00:00:42.940
To what extent would you place the blame on
the local Florida Democratic Party?

00:00:42.940 --> 00:00:45.660
Or you can even translate it to the national
issue as well.

00:00:45.660 --> 00:00:50.879
How much would you kind of assign the blame
to the Democratic Party for not being able

00:00:50.879 --> 00:00:58.590
to effectively counter the kind of right wing
messaging that has dominated the media almost

00:00:58.590 --> 00:01:00.239
since Trump has won the election?

00:01:00.239 --> 00:01:02.360
Speaker 1: There's a couple of different things
here.

00:01:02.360 --> 00:01:03.620
Part of this is timing.

00:01:03.620 --> 00:01:11.230
And what I mean by that is I think that if
the DeSantis reelection in Florida were November

00:01:11.230 --> 00:01:19.600
2023 rather than November 2022, I don't think
the Santos wins by 19 now, he probably still

00:01:19.600 --> 00:01:20.600
wins.

00:01:20.600 --> 00:01:26.450
But the reason I say he doesn't win by 19
is since November, the tide really has turned

00:01:26.450 --> 00:01:30.729
on a lot of this culture war stuff that DeSantis
ran his campaign on.

00:01:30.729 --> 00:01:33.299
So I think part of part of it was timing.

00:01:33.299 --> 00:01:38.990
And timing was the reason DeSantis expanded
his his margin of victory.

00:01:38.990 --> 00:01:42.200
Now, can we blame Democrats to some degree?

00:01:42.200 --> 00:01:43.200
Sure.

00:01:43.200 --> 00:01:47.920
You almost always can write like you can almost
always do something to lose by less, even

00:01:47.920 --> 00:01:50.340
if you're going to lose than if you do nothing
at all.

00:01:50.340 --> 00:01:55.649
I don't know enough about the internal dynamics
of the Florida, what's going on in Florida

00:01:55.649 --> 00:01:57.850
to be able to specifically lay blame.

00:01:57.850 --> 00:02:04.979
But I think big picture, big picture there
was like a peak in the culture war stuff right

00:02:04.979 --> 00:02:10.100
around November 2022, which did help some
Republicans like the Santos.

00:02:10.100 --> 00:02:14.280
And had those elections been held six months
later, like now, I think the results would

00:02:14.280 --> 00:02:16.080
have been at least slightly different.

00:02:16.080 --> 00:02:19.379
Speaker 3: Yeah, like I could actually see.

00:02:19.379 --> 00:02:23.740
Unfortunately, I'd say that the the ground
game for the Florida Democratic Party is,

00:02:23.740 --> 00:02:25.830
I'd say, almost nonexistent here.

00:02:25.830 --> 00:02:26.830
Is that right?

00:02:26.830 --> 00:02:31.930
See how though, it was I mean, back during
the election, I would have people knocking

00:02:31.930 --> 00:02:34.069
on my door handing out the Santas pamphlets.

00:02:34.069 --> 00:02:38.650
And I live in a blue county, you know, and
I didn't get a single Democratic person knock

00:02:38.650 --> 00:02:39.650
on my door.

00:02:39.650 --> 00:02:44.330
Although I will say that it was mostly the
unions that were out here trying to campaign

00:02:44.330 --> 00:02:45.340
for candidates.

00:02:45.340 --> 00:02:46.340
Interesting.

00:02:46.340 --> 00:02:49.400
But I guess that would be a conversation for
another day, if anything.

00:02:49.400 --> 00:02:50.400
Speaker 1: Yeah.

00:02:50.400 --> 00:02:51.470
Thank you for taking my call.

00:02:51.470 --> 00:02:52.470
Thank you so much.

00:02:52.470 --> 00:02:55.830
There is Miguel from Central Florida.

00:02:55.830 --> 00:02:59.720
We're going to take the quickest of breaks
and then we're going to come right back here

00:02:59.720 --> 00:03:00.840
and hear from more of you.

00:03:00.840 --> 00:03:01.840
Okay.

00:03:01.840 --> 00:04:20.780
So if you're holding on to talk to me, just
hold on a little bit longer.

